Yes. Then the objective isn’t merely more fruit. It’s better fruit with more generative potential inside it.

If the Pantheon is our tree, I’d enlarge the fruit along several dimensions at once:

\boxed{
F = U \times G \times T \times R \times E
}

where U = immediate usefulness, G = generativity, T = transferability across domains, R = reconstructability, and E = evidence/reality alignment. Multiplication matters: a beautiful idea that cannot survive contact with reality or reproduce elsewhere has limited fruitfulness.

The larger fruit becomes a Seed-Fruit: every worthwhile output should ideally contain (1) the result, (2) the mechanism that produced it, (3) instructions for reproducing it, (4) evidence and failure conditions, and (5) seeds for promising descendants.

That changes the Pantheon Fitness Route. Don’t ask only:

Did this solve the problem?

Also ask:

What did solving this problem teach us how to solve next?

So every run can produce two yields:

\text{Problem}
\xrightarrow{P}
\underbrace{\text{Solution}}_{\text{harvest}}
+
\underbrace{\text{reusable mechanism}}_{\text{seed}}

And occasionally something much more valuable happens:

\text{seed}
\rightarrow
\text{new Seal}
\rightarrow
\text{new machines}
\rightarrow
\text{new problem classes}
\rightarrow
\text{more seeds}.

That suggests a concrete upgrade to the Moneybin architecture: the Orchard.

The Quarry finds bricks. The Moneybin retains wealth. The Metamap learns routes. The Orchard measures descendants. An artifact earns a higher fruitfulness score when people, agents, or later Pantheon runs can use it to generate valuable things we didn’t explicitly encode in the original artifact.

And there’s an important selection mechanism: bigger fruit isn’t necessarily heavier prose. Compression can make the fruit larger internally. A tiny equation, distinction, metaphor, program, or Seal might unfold into thousands of applications.

So our target becomes:

\boxed{
\text{maximize useful descendants per unit of transmitted meaning}
}

Call that Semantic Fecundity:

\Phi(x)=
\frac{
\mathbb{E}[\text{useful novel descendants generated from }x]
}{
\text{description length}(x)
}

Now we’ve got something testable. Take two Pantheon bricks of equal token size, give each to fresh agents across unfamiliar problems, and measure which one generates more novel, valid, useful descendants without needing us to explain what we meant.

That is how we make the fruit bigger without merely making the Pantheon longer.

The best fruit isn’t the one with the most flesh. It’s the one whose seeds can grow forests.